---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import ControlStrip from '../components/ControlStrip.astro';
import DownloadCards from '../components/DownloadCards.astro';
import Feature from '../components/Feature.astro';
import InstallCommand from '../components/InstallCommand.astro';
import SectionHead from '../components/SectionHead.astro';
import { base } from '../lib/base.js';
import { products } from '../lib/marks.js';
import { download, downloads as loadDownloads, github, marketplace, pages, setupYaml, workbenchNav } from '../lib/workbench.js';
// Written by screenshots/capture.cjs: the overview's numbered regions and
// the controls strip's crops and hotspots, as percentages of each image.
import screenshots from '../data/screenshots.json';

const downloads = await loadDownloads();
const version = downloads && downloads.version;
const mark = products.find(p => p.name === 'Workbench');

const statesYaml = `- label: Sign in
  src: pages/sign-in.html
  states:
    - id: default
      label: Default
    - id: error
      label: Wrong password`;
const docsMarkdown = [
  '# Card',
  '',
  'A plain bordered surface that groups related content.',
  '',
  '## Basic',
  '',
  '```example basic',
  'caption: children',
  '```',
  '',
  'The surface alone.',
].join('\n');

---
<Base
  title="Canonic Workbench"
  description="A VS Code extension that renders a project's pages and component previews on one canvas at device widths, with annotations and screenshots for coding agents."
  product="Workbench"
  version={version}
  nav={workbenchNav('Overview')}
  github={github}
  download={download}
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="hero-text">
        <a class="chip" href="#tour">
          <svg viewBox={mark.viewBox} aria-hidden="true"><path d={mark.base} fill="currentColor" /><path d={mark.accent} class="accent" /></svg>
          Canonic Workbench{version && ` v${version}`}
        </a>
        <h1>Preview your project’s pages and components in VS Code.</h1>
        <p>A VS Code extension that renders HTML pages, TypeScript component previews, Storybook stories, and running apps on one canvas at device widths. You can annotate a page and copy the annotations, with a screenshot, as a prompt for a coding agent.</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="btn" href="#install">Install</a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="#tour">Interface</a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={pages.docs}>Docs</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <figure class="window">
        <div class="window-bar" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="dot"></span><span class="dot"></span><span class="dot"></span>
          <span class="addr">#pages/sign-in.html</span>
        </div>
        <div class="shot pinned">
          <img src={`${base}images/overview.jpg`} width="1440" height="860" alt="Workbench: a page list on the left with an Auth group and a Sign in page, and the Acme sign-in page on the canvas at full width.">
          {screenshots.regions.map(region => (
            <>
              <span class="region" style={`left: ${region.box.left}%; top: ${region.box.top}%; width: ${region.box.width}%; height: ${region.box.height}%`} aria-hidden="true"></span>
              <span class="pin" style={`left: ${region.pin.left}%; top: ${region.pin.top}%`} title={region.name} aria-hidden="true">{region.n}</span>
            </>
          ))}
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="tour" class="tour band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="controls-block">
        <SectionHead kicker="Interface" title="Layout">
          Workbench reads <code>workbench.yaml</code> at the root of your project. The sidebar lists the pages it declares, the TypeScript previews it finds, and the pages from any implementation catalogs you enable, such as a Storybook. Matching collection and group names bring those pages together in one list. Its bundled native compiler caches unchanged preview builds across restarts.
        </SectionHead>
        <ol class="areas">
          <li><span class="n">01</span><h3>Page list</h3><p>The collections in <code>workbench.yaml</code>, such as Pages and Components, at the top; the selected collection’s groups and pages below; search at the bottom. A page’s states are listed under it. In VS Code the list is in the sidebar, under a switcher for the spaces you’ve added.</p></li>
          <li><span class="n">02</span><h3>Top bar</h3><p>The page and its state on the left. The lens switcher, the size switcher, and the Actions switch in the middle; the page’s actions on the right. Controls that don’t fit move into the More menu.</p></li>
          <li><span class="n">03</span><h3>Canvas</h3><p>The page, rendered live in an artboard of the selected size. Zoom and pan change the view; the page keeps its layout width. The current page stays visible while the next one loads.</p></li>
          <li><span class="n">04</span><h3>Toolbar</h3><p>Annotation tools and the handoff, under the artboard. Select returns to using the page.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <div class="controls-block" id="controls">
        <SectionHead kicker="Top bar and toolbar" title="Controls">
          The size switcher, the Actions switch, and the page’s actions are in the top bar. The toolbar’s annotation tools and the view controls are at the bottom of the canvas. Point at or tap a control to see what it does.
        </SectionHead>

        <ControlStrip groups={screenshots.controls}>
          <div class="controls-groups">
            <div class="controls-col">
              <div class="controls-group">
                <h3>Interaction</h3>
                <dl class="controls" data-group="actions">
                  <div data-label="Actions — let links navigate and forms submit"><dt>Actions</dt><dd>Off by default: links and form submissions are logged instead of followed. Turn it on to let links navigate and forms submit.</dd></div>
                </dl>
              </div>
              <div class="controls-group">
                <h3>Only in some setups</h3>
                <dl class="controls">
                  <div><dt>Space switcher</dt><dd>At the top of the VS Code sidebar, and in the canvas breadcrumb when several spaces are available. Pick a space with the pointer or arrow keys; Escape closes the menu and Tab moves to the next control.</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>State switcher</dt><dd>Beside the page’s name in the top bar when it has states, or under a Storybook lens, listing the title’s stories. Story picks reuse the loaded preview.</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>Lens switcher</dt><dd>The authored page followed by additional implementations declared in <code>workbench.yaml</code>. Every lens label is customizable; defaults are Workbench for TypeScript previews and Design for HTML pages. See <a href="#lenses">Lenses</a>.</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>Preview controls</dt><dd>For Workbench previews: edit inputs, reset the selected state, inspect actions, and read documentation. Changes stay temporary.</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>Copy handoff</dt><dd>In VS Code only, at the end of the toolbar, beside a count of your annotations. See <a href="#annotations">Annotations and handoff</a>.</dd></div>
                </dl>
              </div>
              <div class="controls-group">
                <h3>View controls</h3>
                <dl class="controls" data-group="view">
                  <div data-label="Recenter view"><dt>Recenter view</dt><dd>Brings the artboard back to the middle of the canvas at the current zoom level.</dd></div>
                  <div data-label="Zoom out"><dt>Zoom out</dt><dd>Halves the zoom, to the next power of two. <kbd>⌘−</kbd> or <kbd>Ctrl −</kbd>.</dd></div>
                  <div data-label="Zoom options"><dt>Zoom level</dt><dd>Opens zoom to fit, zoom to 100%, 50%, and 200%.</dd></div>
                  <div data-label="Zoom in"><dt>Zoom in</dt><dd>Doubles the zoom, to the next power of two. <kbd>⌘+</kbd> or <kbd>Ctrl +</kbd>.</dd></div>
                </dl>
                <p class="controls-note">As in Figma: <kbd>⌘</kbd> or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> with the scroll wheel, or a pinch, zooms at the pointer. Scroll to pan, or hold <kbd>Space</kbd> and drag. <kbd>⌘0</kbd> or <kbd>⇧0</kbd> zooms to 100%; <kbd>⇧1</kbd> zooms to fit. A new size fits itself to the window until you zoom or pan.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="controls-group">
              <h3>Annotations</h3>
              <dl class="controls" data-group="annotations">
                <div data-label="Select"><dt>Select</dt><dd>Use the page, and pick or move annotations. <kbd>Esc</kbd> returns here from any drawing tool.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Scribble"><dt>Scribble</dt><dd>Freehand drawing.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Arrow"><dt>Arrow</dt><dd>An arrow.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Rectangle"><dt>Shapes</dt><dd>Rectangle by default. The small arrow beside it switches to a line or a circle.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Text"><dt>Text</dt><dd>A label placed on the page.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Comment"><dt>Comment</dt><dd>A pinned note.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Undo last annotation"><dt>Undo</dt><dd>Removes the last annotation. <kbd>⌘Z</kbd> or <kbd>Ctrl Z</kbd>.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Clear annotations"><dt>Clear annotations</dt><dd>Removes every annotation on the page.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Save screenshot"><dt>Save screenshot</dt><dd>Downloads a JPEG of the page with your annotations on it.</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </div>
            <div class="controls-group">
              <h3>Size and page</h3>
              <dl class="controls" data-group="page">
                <div data-label="Fit"><dt>Fit</dt><dd>The artboard fills whatever space the workbench has.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Laptop, MacBook Pro 14, 1512 by 982 points"><dt>Laptop</dt><dd>1512 × 982, a 14-inch MacBook Pro. The canvas zooms out to show all of it; the page still lays out at its full width.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Mobile, iPhone 15 Pro, 393 by 852 points"><dt>Mobile</dt><dd>393 × 852, an iPhone 15 Pro.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Resizable"><dt>Resizable</dt><dd>Drag an outside edge or corner to any size. The artboard keeps that size until you change it.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Reload"><dt>Reload</dt><dd>Reloads the page.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Open the source"><dt>Open the source</dt><dd>Lists the page’s design file and any implementation code, and opens one in the editor.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Copy reference"><dt>Copy reference</dt><dd>Copies a short text reference to the page, state, and lens, for pasting into a conversation. It doesn’t include a screenshot or your annotations.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Open on its own"><dt>Open on its own</dt><dd>Opens the page directly in a browser tab.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="More"><dt>More (⋯)</dt><dd><b>Configure pages</b>: a form for adding and removing collections and pages, editing labels and paths, and choosing each page’s sizes; it rewrites only the <code>collections</code> part of <code>workbench.yaml</code>. <b>Download design-system ZIP</b>: every page’s files, the sources they depend on, and a reference screenshot at each size, in parts of 10 MB or less. When the window is narrow, the controls that don’t fit move here too.</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </div>
          </div>
        </ControlStrip>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="features">
    <div class="wrap">
      <Feature id="states" kicker="States" title="Pages with several states">
          <p>A page can have several states, such as a sign-in form when empty and after a wrong password. States are listed under their page, like stories under a component in Storybook.</p>
          <p>An HTML page reads its state from <code>html[data-wb-state]</code>. Match CSS on <code>html[data-wb-state="error"]</code>, keep an element only in some states with <code>data-wb-state-only="error"</code>, or set a value with <code>data-wb-set-error="value=…"</code>. No scripts are added to your pages.</p>
          <CodeBlock file="workbench.yaml" code={statesYaml} copies="the states example" />
        <figure class="feature-media shot" slot="media">
          <img src={`${base}images/states.jpg`} width="1120" height="860" loading="lazy" alt="The Wrong password state of the sign-in page at mobile width: an error message above filled-in fields, with the password field outlined in red.">
        </figure>
      </Feature>

      <Feature id="annotations" kicker="Annotations and handoff" title="Annotate a page and copy it for an agent">
          <p>Boxes, arrows, scribbles, and pinned comments are drawn over the running page, which stays usable with Select. Select an annotation to move it, nudge it with the arrow keys, or delete it with <kbd>Delete</kbd>.</p>
          <p>In VS Code, <b>Copy handoff</b> saves a screenshot to the project’s ignored <code>.canonic/.handoffs/</code> folder, clears the annotations, and copies a text description of each annotation to the clipboard: the element under it and the page’s source file. Paste it into a coding agent’s chat.</p>
        <figure class="feature-media shot" slot="media">
          <img src={`${base}images/annotations.jpg`} width="1120" height="860" loading="lazy" alt="The error state with annotations: a red box around the error message, an arrow pointing to it, and a wavy line under the password field.">
        </figure>
      </Feature>

      <Feature id="lenses" kicker="Lenses" title="TypeScript previews and lenses">
          <p>Define components and pages in <code>.workbench.ts</code> files. Built-in adapters render HTML, React, Vue, Astro, and React Native Web, and you can register others. Each preview has named states, editable inputs, and an action log. A page can also show its implementation as a lens: a Storybook story, a dev or staging URL, the iOS Simulator, or a macOS app window.</p>
          <p>Each state gives the preview its data: props, providers from a project-wide environment, or mocked responses to the page’s <code>fetch</code> and XMLHttpRequest calls. Links can open other previews; other navigation, such as a download or a route without a preview, is logged as an action. See <a href={`${pages.docs}preview-data/#choose-how-to-give-a-page-its-data`}>Give a page its data</a>.</p>
          <p>The selected lens stays as you change pages and is part of the address, so a copied link opens the same view. You can choose sidebar icons for TypeScript previews individually or by title prefix, and give a shared collection its own icon. SVG icon sprites in TypeScript previews remain available as you switch states or return to a page. For a Storybook or local server, you can configure a start command and a readiness check; in a trusted workspace, Workbench runs the command in a terminal when the check fails. The design-system export includes a static viewer for the previews; Astro previews export their authored states only. If the viewer cannot be built, the export still delivers sources and reference screenshots with a warning.</p>
          <img src={`${base}images/workbench-previews.jpg`} alt="Acme TypeScript preview with its named states and editable input controls" loading="lazy" />
        <div class="feature-media" slot="media">
          <CodeBlock file="address" code="#pages/sign-in.html:error@393~staging" copies="the address example" />
          <table class="address">
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><code>pages/sign-in.html</code></td><td>the page’s design file</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>:error</code></td><td>the state, left out for the default</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>@393</code></td><td>the size: a device width, <code>fit</code>, or a resizable size</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>~staging</code></td><td>the lens, left out for the design</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </Feature>
      <Feature id="docs" kicker="Docs pages" title="Component documentation with live examples">
          <p>A page written in Markdown documents a component or a part of a design system: sections, notes, hand-written props tables, and live examples rendered from your own source files. <b>Show code</b> under an example shows its source. A docs page shown on its own fills the canvas, on white, and scrolls like a web page.</p>
          <p>Lenses render the same examples in different ways, such as React and React Native Web. Annotations, handoffs, and the design-system export work on docs pages too. See <a href={`${pages.docs}docs-pages/`}>Docs pages</a>.</p>
        <div class="feature-media" slot="media">
          <CodeBlock file="docs/card.md" code={docsMarkdown} copies="the docs page example" />
        </div>
      </Feature>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="setup" class="setup ink">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="setup-text">
        <span class="kicker">Setup</span>
        <h2>Configuration</h2>
        <p class="lede">Workbench activates in a folder with a <code>workbench.yaml</code> at its root. Your pages and preview definitions stay in your project; the extension adds nothing to them.</p>
      </div>
      <CodeBlock file="workbench.yaml" code={setupYaml} copies="the workbench.yaml example" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="install" class="install">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="install-head">
        <SectionHead kicker={`Install${version ? ` · v${version}` : ''}`} title="Install">
          In VS Code, search for <b>Canonic Workbench</b> in the Extensions view, or see it on the <a href={marketplace} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visual Studio Marketplace ↗</a>. For Cursor, Windsurf, and other VS Code forks, install the <code>.vsix</code> for your platform{downloads ? ':' : ' from GitHub.'}
        </SectionHead>
        <a class="text-link" href={pages.install}>Install guide →</a>
      </div>

      <DownloadCards downloads={downloads} />

      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <span class="n">01</span>
          <span class="title">Install a .vsix</span>
          <p>Only needed outside the Marketplace. In the Extensions view, click <b>⋯</b> and choose <b>Install from VSIX…</b>, or run:</p>
          <InstallCommand />
        </li>
        <li><span class="n">02</span><span class="title">Reload the window</span><p>If the editor was already open, run <b>Developer: Reload Window</b> from the Command Palette.</p></li>
        <li><span class="n">03</span><span class="title">Add workbench.yaml</span><p>Create it at the root of your project, like the <a href="#setup">example above</a>.</p></li>
        <li><span class="n">04</span><span class="title">Open the canvas</span><p>Open the <b>Workbench</b> view in the activity bar and pick a page.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Fragment slot="footer"><span>Pages shown are an example project.</span></Fragment>
</Base>
